##DESCRIPTION
##  Problem that asks about doing arithmetic mentally.
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('teacher preparation', 'mental math', 'arithmetic')

## DBsubject('Teacher Preparation')
## DBchapter('Basic Arithmetic')
## DBsection('Mental Mathematics')
## Date('8/30/2020')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')
# updated 2023.08.20

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl",);

Context("Numeric");

$b = random(3, 5);
$r = random(1, $b - 1);
do {
    $q = random(132, 300);
    $a = $b * $q + $r;
} until ($a < 1000);

@values = split(//, $a);
$a2     = $values[0];
$a1     = $values[1];
$a0     = $values[2];

$q2 = int(floor($a2 / $b));
$r2 = $a2 % $b;

$q1 = int(floor((10 * $r2 + $a1) / $b));
$r1 = (10 * $r2 + $a1) % $b;

$q0 = int(floor((10 * $r1 + $a0) / $b));
$r0 = (10 * $r1 + $a0) % $b;

BEGIN_PGML

This problem asks you to do a chip model with division.  Consider the divsion problem [`[$a] \div [$b]`].  Start with [$a] on a chip model with appropriate columns. We will then put these chips into [$b] equal rows.

1. Make [$b] empty rows on the diagram.
2. Move the chips in the hundreds column to [$b] equal rows.  You can put [___]{$q2} into each row and there will be [___]{$r2} hundreds chips left over.

3. Unbundle any leftover hundreds chips into the empty rows of tens column.  Then move the original tens chips into the empty rows until there are equal numbers in each.  There are [___]{$q1} tens chips in each row and there are [___]{$r1} left over.

4. Unbundle any leftover tens chips into the empty rows of the ones column.  Then move the original ones chips into the empty rows until there are equal numbers in each.  There are [___]{$q0} ones chips in each row and there are [___]{$r0} left over.


This shows that [`[$a] \div [$b]=`] [___]{$q} R [___]{$r}

END_PGML

$a10 = 10 * $r2 + $a1;
$a00 = 10 * $r1 + $a0;

$step2 =
    $r2 == 0
    ? "Since there are no hundreds chips remaining, we just use the $a1 tens chips."
    : "There are $r2 hundreds chips remaining, if we unbundle these with the original $a1 chips, we have $a10 chips";

$step3 =
    $r1 == 0
    ? "Since there are no tens chips remaining, we just use the $a0 ones chips."
    : "There are $r1 tens chips remaining, if we unbundle these with the original $a0 chips, we have $a00 chips";

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
This is difficut to show visually here, but here is a description of a chip model of [`[$a] \div [$b]`].

1. There are [$a2] hundreds chips and we can make [$b] equal rows of size [$q2] with [$r2] left over. 

2. [$step2] With these tens, we can make [$b] equal rows of size [$q1] with [$r1] left over. 

3. [$step3] With these ones chips, we can make [$b] equal rows of size [$q0] with [$r0] left over. 

The result then shows that [`[$a] \div [$b] = [$q] \; R \; [$r]`]


END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
